# graphy — code knowledge graph

This repository has a pre-built knowledge graph (`.graphy/`) of its code:
functions, methods, types, docs, and their typed relationships (calls,
imports, inherits, implements, references, contains, defines, explains), each
tagged EXTRACTED, INFERRED, or AMBIGUOUS by resolution confidence.

It also records *why* the code is the way it is: `rationale` nodes, extracted
from marked comments (WHY:, RATIONALE:, DECISION:, HACK:, FIXME:, TODO:) and
from architecture decision records, linked to what they justify by `explains`
edges. Check an entity's rationale before changing unfamiliar code.

Prefer graph queries over file scanning for structural questions — one query
replaces many file reads.

## Commands

- `graphy query "<question>"` — natural-language question; returns the
  relevant subgraph. Works offline (keyword mode) without an API key.
- `graphy path <a> <b>` — shortest connection between two entities.
- `graphy explain <entity>` — entity card: location, signature, doc,
  community, and every in/out edge.
- `graphy report` — GRAPH_REPORT.md: stats, god nodes (flow hubs),
  detected communities, recorded rationale. Read this first for architecture
  overviews.

Asking "why does X work this way?" surfaces the rationale attached to X,
offline, because rationale prose is keyword-searchable.

Entities are matched by name (`CreateConcept`), qualified name
(`Store.PutFile`), or id; ambiguous names list candidates.

## Freshness

After editing code, run `graphy update` (incremental; sub-second) so
queries reflect your changes.

## MCP alternative

`graphy mcp` serves the same capabilities as MCP stdio tools
(graphy_query, graphy_path, graphy_explain, graphy_rationale, graphy_report,
graphy_update).
